    :this-adr-broke-its-own-rule \"2026-08-15: an earlier revision of :adr/body contained the prose 10 < 4 written with bare double quotes around the numerals. That TERMINATED the body string early. The reader did not error -- edn/read-string returned a map, the two stray numerals became entity keys 10 and 4, and ~1.5 KB of the body (the whole landed-work section and the next-steps list) was parsed as trailing garbage instead of text. A verification step that printed 'body chars 7810' looked entirely plausible and was accepted. This is the exact shape ADR-2608136000 describes, inside the document that cites it: the file READS while being wrong, so nothing downstream complains. The check that would have caught it is not 'does it parse' but 'does the entity have exactly the expected keys, and does the body still contain the sections it is supposed to'. Escape quotes inside an :adr/body, or use corner brackets.\"}"}]
